  "system": {
    "description": "Aquanauts are environmentally adapted for underwater activities while still functional on land. Their heart rate slows while submerged, their skin includes a layer of blubber that retains heat, they store oxygen in their muscle tissue, and they do not suffer negative health effects from pressure changes. Additionally, their eyes have nictitating membranes and their corneas adjust to counter underwater refraction. Their hands and feet are webbed and they possess a transgenic swim bladder for controlling buoyancy.",
